+# The Daily Grind
+
+Run `pnpm install` and `npm run build` to type-check the sign-in client and bundle the homepage into `dist/`. Serve that output through the application's same-origin HTTPS server. The start script previews the built output.
+
+The homepage intercepts ordinary clicks on Rewards, My Account, Order Now, and Sign in. It prepares capabilities and request options without prompting while the visitor browses. When a click occurs before preparation completes or after the challenge expires, it opens the inline sign-in dialog. Otherwise it requests the unified immediate passkey/password chooser directly during the click. Modified clicks preserve normal link behavior.
+
+## Authentication service contract
+
+This repository contains a static homepage, not an authentication server or user database. The deployment must supply these same-origin endpoints; the client never treats a local credential alone as a verified sign-in:
+
+- `POST /api/login/options`: Return WebAuthn request options JSON with a cryptographically random Base64URL challenge, the deployment's `rpId`, `userVerification: "preferred"`, and no credential allowlist. Bind the challenge, expiry, and expected verification preference to the visitor's server session. Allow this endpoint before authentication. Return a fresh challenge on each call.
+- `POST /api/login/verify`: Accept the assertion's `toJSON()` representation. Verify the session-bound, unexpired, single-use challenge, origin, RP ID, signature, and verification preference against the stored credential and public key. Return 404 only for an unknown credential ID, allowing the client to signal its removal when supported. Establish the authenticated session only after successful verification.
+- `POST /api/login/password`: Accept JSON `{ "username": "…", "password": "…" }`. Validate against the account database and establish the same session on success; return 401 for invalid credentials.
+
+Both verification endpoints must return a 2xx response only after establishing the authenticated session using a secure HttpOnly cookie. Apply same-origin/CSRF protection, rate limiting, and non-cacheable responses. Never log credential secrets. Render `data-signed-in="true"` on the body for an existing verified session; protected destinations must also enforce the server session. `/logout` remains the server's logout route. Without these services the homepage shows sign-in errors rather than simulating authentication.
+
+Successful contextual sign-in continues to the original link destination. Signing in from the header leaves the customer on the homepage. Closing the fallback dialog retains the browsing page. The fallback passkey button uses the standard modal request so external security keys and cross-device passkeys remain available; password inputs support browser autofill. No credential requests run on page load.
